INT. AMBULANCE - MOVING - NIGHT

A gridlocked highway. Red tail lights stretch to the horizon. Rain. The ambulance siren whoops, goes silent, whoops again. It is not moving.

CYRUS BEAUMONT-OKAFOR (30s, paramedic, reflective vest, steady hands) kneels beside a stretcher. On it lies a WOMAN (60s, oxygen mask, silver hair plastered to her forehead, a rosary wound around one wrist).

CYRUS
Ma'am, stay with me.

The woman's lips move behind the mask.

CYRUS (CONT'D)
Say again?

WOMAN
Pain.

CYRUS
Where?

WOMAN
Here.

She presses a shaking hand against her sternum. Cyrus checks the monitor. The line jumps. He tears open a packet.

CYRUS
Aspirin. Chew.

She chews. Winces. He adjusts the oxygen.

A horn blares from the cab, long and helpless. Cyrus slams his fist on the partition.

CYRUS (CONT'D)
Anything?

Nothing. Rain beats the roof.

He turns back to the woman. Her eyes are glassy.

CYRUS (CONT'D)
What's your name?

WOMAN
Odile.

CYRUS
Odile. I'm Cyrus. We'll get you there.

ODILE
(whispering)
You're kind.

CYRUS
I'm stuck.

ODILE
Both.

The monitor chirps. Cyrus grabs the paddles, then lets them fall back. Her rhythm steadies, ragged but present.

He grips her hand. She grips his. The rosary beads dig into both their palms.

CYRUS (CONT'D)
Odile, listen. If you can hear me, squeeze.

She squeezes.

Outside, the red lights ahead begin to slide apart, and a lane opens.

The ambulance lurches forward.
